WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.440
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:09.849
Reinforcement learning is a learning algorithm
that chooses a set of actions in an environment

00:00:09.849 --> 00:00:11.530
to maximize a score.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:18.040
This class of techniques enables us to train
an AI to master video games, avoiding obstacles

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:24.520
with a drone, cleaning up a table with a robot
arm, and has many more really cool applications.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.370
We use the word score and reward interchangeably,
and the goal is that over time, the agent

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.820
has to learn to maximize a prescribed reward.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.289
So where should the rewards come from?

00:00:36.289 --> 00:00:39.989
Most techniques work by using extrinsic rewards.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.309
Extrinsic rewards are only a half-solution
as they need to come from somewhere, either

00:00:44.309 --> 00:00:49.370
from the game in the form of a game score,
which simply isn't present in every game.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:53.879
And even if it is present in a game, it is
very different for Atari breakout and for

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:56.329
instance, a strategy game.

00:00:56.329 --> 00:01:01.239
Intrinsic rewards are designed to come to
the rescue, so the AI would be able to completely

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:07.159
ignore the in-game score and somehow have
some sort of inner motivation to drive an

00:01:07.159 --> 00:01:08.719
AI to complete a level.

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:13.920
But what could possibly be a good intrinsic
reward that would work well on a variety of

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.060
tasks?

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.460
Shouldn't this be different from problem to
problem?

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.149
If so, we are back to square one.

00:01:20.149 --> 00:01:25.520
If we are to call our learner intelligent,
then we need one algorithm that is able to

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.300
solve a large number of different problems.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:32.460
If we need to reprogram it for every game,
that's just a narrow intelligence.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:39.329
So, a key finding of this paper is that we
can endow the AI with a very human-like property

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:41.390
- curiosity.

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:46.920
Human babies also explore the world out of
curiosity and as a happy side-effect, learn

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.320
a lot of useful skills to navigate in this
world later.

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:56.000
However, as in our everyday speech, the definition
of curiosity is a little nebulous, we have

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.040
to provide a mathematical definition for it.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.170
In this work, this is defined as trying to
maximize the number of surprises.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:09.420
This will drive the learner to favor actions
that lead to unexplored regions and complex

00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.090
dynamics in a game.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.030
So, how do these curious agents fare?

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.970
Well, quite good!

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:21.510
In Pong, when the agent plays against itself,
it will end up in long matches passing the

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:25.110
ball between the two paddles.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:26.270
How about bowling?

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:30.409
Well, I cannot resist but quote the authors
for this one.

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:36.209
The agent learned to play the game better
than agents trained to maximize the (clipped)

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:37.970
extrinsic reward directly.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:43.459
We think this is because the agent gets attracted
to the difficult-to-predict flashing of the

00:02:43.459 --> 00:02:46.569
scoreboard occurring after the strikes.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:52.269
With a little stretch one could perhaps say
that this AI is showing signs of addiction.

00:02:52.269 --> 00:02:56.410
I wonder how it would do with modern mobile
games with loot boxes?

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:59.099
But, we'll leave that for future work now.

00:02:59.099 --> 00:03:00.560
How about Super Mario?

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.180
Well, the agent is very curious to see how
the levels continue, so it learns all the

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.760
necessary skills to beat the game.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.760
Incredible.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.390
However, the more seasoned Fellow Scholars
immediately find that there is a catch.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:21.890
What if we sit down the AI in front of a TV
that constantly plays new material?

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:25.120
You may think this is some kind of a joke,
but it's not.

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:30.280
It is a perfectly valid issue, because due
to its curiosity, the AI would have to stay

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:33.970
there forever and not start exploring the
level.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.110
This is the good old definition of TV addiction.

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.280
Talk about humanlike properties.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.530
And sure enough, as soon as we turn off the
TV, the agent gets to work immediately.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.799
Who would have thought!

00:03:45.799 --> 00:03:50.940
The paper notes that this challenge needs
to be dealt with over time, however, the algorithm

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.840
was tested on a large variety of problems,
and it did not come up in practice.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:01.579
And the key insight is that curiosity is not
only a great replacement for extrinsic rewards,

00:04:01.579 --> 00:04:07.160
the two are often aligned, but curiosity,
in some cases, is even superior to that.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:13.450
That is an amazing value proposition for something
that we can run on any problem without any

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.970
additional work.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:19.940
So, curious agents that are addicted to flashing
score screens and TVs.

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:21.880
What a time to be alive!

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.960
And, if you enjoyed this episode and you wish
